---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const matSelCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Translate: design → material requirements', note: '"Light, stiff, cheap, survives 80 °C" becomes property targets: density, modulus, cost per volume, service temperature. Translation is where design engineering and materials engineering shake hands.' },
    { math: '2 · Screening and ranking', note: 'Screen the universe of materials (~100,000 in databases) against hard limits; rank survivors by a performance index — e.g. E^½/ρ for a light stiff beam: the maths of which property combination wins for which job.' },
    { math: '3 · Ashby charts make the trade visible', note: 'Plot property against property (strength vs density, modulus vs cost) on log axes — each material class occupies a bubble. The best material for "light and strong" is the one the 45° line touches first. The whole discipline on one chart.' },
    { math: '4 · Shape, processing and documentation', note: 'Section shape multiplies performance (an I-beam beats a rod of the same mass); processing constrains choice (castable? weldable?); documentation seals the audit trail. The CES selector software industrialised Ashby\'s method.' },
  ],
};

const matSelQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'For a light, stiff beam in bending, the winning performance index is…', choices: ['E/ρ', 'E^½/ρ', 'ρ/E', 'σy/ρ'], answer: 1, why: 'Deriving from beam stiffness (S depends on E·A²) and mass (on ρ·A), minimising mass at fixed stiffness maximises √E/ρ. In tension it would be E/ρ — the geometry of the loading changes the exponent, which is why translation matters.' },
    { q: 'On the Ashby modulus–density chart, the guideline line E^½/ρ = constant has slope…', choices: ['1', '2', '½', '0'], answer: 1, why: 'log E = 2·log ρ + constant — a slope-2 line on log axes. Materials above the line beat the benchmark; sliding the line across the bubbles ranks every material in one picture.' },
    { q: 'A "hard limit" in Ashby screening differs from a "ranking objective" because…', choices: ['limits are cheaper', 'a limit disqualifies (must survive 300 °C, must not corrode); the objective orders the survivors (minimise mass, cost, carbon)', 'limits are for metals only', 'there is no difference'], answer: 1, why: 'Screening is a pass/fail filter on constraints; ranking is an optimisation over whatever passes. Confusing the two either kills viable candidates or optimises something that will fail.' },
    { q: 'Why does wood compete with carbon fibre for light stiff beams, but not for light stiff ties (tension rods)?', choices: ['wood is stronger in tension', 'the index changes: bending rewards E^½/ρ (where wood\'s low density shines), tension rewards E/ρ (where it does not)', 'wood is cheaper', 'it doesn\'t — wood is always worse'], answer: 1, why: 'Same materials, different loading geometry, different exponent on E. The index framework is what makes the "it depends" of materials selection computable.' },
  ],
};

const msShockPlace = {
  "prompt": "A surface suddenly cooled cracks when the induced stress reaches σ_f — at ΔT_c ≈ σ_f/(E·α). Soda-lime glass manages about 70 K. Where does borosilicate sit?",
  "labels": [
    "0",
    "75 K",
    "150 K",
    "225 K",
    "300 K"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 300
  },
  "answer": 240,
  "tolerance": 20,
  "why": "Borosilicate (α ≈ 3.3 × 10⁻⁶) survives ΔT ≈ 240 K where soda-lime (α ≈ 9 × 10⁻⁶) manages about 70 K — which is why ovenware is borosilicate and windows are not; fused silica (α ≈ 0.5 × 10⁻⁶) survives the extreme end."
};

const msWireMatch = {
  "prompt": "Same database, different question — pair the basis with its winner:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "conductivity per cubic metre",
      "right": "copper — 59 against aluminium's 38 MS/m"
    },
    {
      "left": "conductivity per kilogram",
      "right": "aluminium — more than twice the current per unit mass"
    },
    {
      "left": "conductivity per unit cost",
      "right": "house wiring"
    },
    {
      "left": "strength added to the conductor",
      "right": "steel-cored aluminium — the overhead line"
    }
  ],
  "why": "A selection compares the property combination the objective asks for — the database answers only the question you write down."
};

const msIndexOrder = {
  "prompt": "From beam theory to the performance index — put the derivation in order:",
  "items": [
    "Beam theory: stiffness S = C·E·I/L³",
    "Geometrically similar sections: I scales with A²",
    "Solve for the section: A = √(S·L³/(C·E))",
    "Mass: m = ρ·A·L",
    "Minimising mass means maximising M = E^½/ρ"
  ],
  "why": "Everything except ρ and E is fixed by the specification — and a tension tie gives M = E/ρ, a panel in bending E^⅓/ρ: the exponent encodes the loading geometry."
};

const msRankBuckets = {
  "prompt": "Worked ranking, (GPa)^½ per (Mg/m³) — file each material by its number:",
  "buckets": [
    "M ≈ 6 — the light-stiff elite",
    "M ≈ 2–3 — the metals"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "carbon-fibre composite, 6.5",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "pine along the grain, 6.3",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "aluminium, 3.1",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "titanium, 2.4",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "mild steel, 1.9",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "The numbers are (GPa)^½ per (Mg/m³); change the objective to cost per unit stiffness and steel surges back — the winner is a property of the question."
};

const msBubbleMatch = {
  "prompt": "The point cloud organises into class bubbles — pair class with position:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "ceramics",
      "right": "top-right — stiff, dense-ish"
    },
    {
      "left": "foams",
      "right": "bottom-left — compliant, light"
    },
    {
      "left": "elastomers",
      "right": "far bottom-left"
    },
    {
      "left": "metals",
      "right": "a diagonal band across the middle"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Slide the guideline across the chart toward the top-left and the first bubble it touches is the winner — an entire ranking performed as geometry, with property envelopes keeping the scatter honest (ceramics' strength spans two decades)."
};

const msShapeEstimate = {
  "prompt": "How many times the bending stiffness of a solid rod of the same mass does a well-shaped I-beam or box section carry?",
  "unit": "× the stiffness",
  "answer": 17,
  "factor": 1.7,
  "why": "About 10–25 times — so \"steel, shaped well\" can beat \"aluminium, shaped badly\"; local instability of thin walls is what sets the ceiling.",
  "context": "Shape and process are multipliers: process compatibility (castable, weldable, extrudable, printable) and the audit trail of documentation complete the decision."
};

const msFrameBranch = {
  "root": "One pass of the method, on a showroom object — make the calls.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "A bicycle frame: a set of ties and beams in bending and torsion; objective, minimum mass at acceptable cost. Screening — which candidates die first?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Thermoplastics (creep), ceramics (toughness), lead (weight)",
          "next": "rank",
          "feedback": "The hard limits do the killing before any ranking begins."
        },
        {
          "label": "Steel, aluminium and titanium — too ordinary",
          "next": "rank",
          "feedback": "Those are the survivors — the ranking happens to them, not on them."
        },
        {
          "label": "Whatever fails a price cap only",
          "next": "rank",
          "feedback": "A price cap is one constraint among several — decades of fatigue, no rust, joinable, serviceable to −20 °C all bite too."
        }
      ]
    },
    "rank": {
      "text": "Ranking on E^½/ρ — who tops the list?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "CFRP, titanium and aluminium, steel below",
          "next": "market",
          "feedback": "Exactly the ranking the index produces — and the showroom confirms it."
        },
        {
          "label": "Steel first — cheapest per tonne",
          "next": "market",
          "feedback": "Cheap is a different objective; on mass-per-stiffness steel sits below the light metals."
        }
      ]
    },
    "market": {
      "text": "Carbon-fibre racing frames, aluminium mid-range, titanium boutique, steel touring — a translation the buyers made without ever writing it down. Documentation: the layup schedule, the tube supplier's certificate."
    }
  },
  "why": "One pass of the method, and the showroom becomes a chart you can read."
};

const msVesselSpot = {
  "prompt": "An engineer reasons through a pressure vessel. One line is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Mass minimisation: maximise σ_y/ρ — the wall as thin as the yield strength allows",
    "Safety: maximise K_IC/σ_y — the vessel must yield before it breaks",
    "Ultra-high-strength steel wins both indices, so it is the choice",
    "The professional answer: a middle-strength, high-toughness steel — or titanium when mass is precious (spacecraft tanks)"
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Ultra-high-strength steel wins the first index and fails the second — its toughness is poor precisely because its strength is high.",
  "why": "A design with two failure modes needs two indices, and the candidate set is the region where both are satisfied — failure-mode thinking stops the method ranking confidently into catastrophe."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/advanced/materials-selection">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Engineering lesson, and it spends everything the earlier
    tiers banked. From the foundational tier you need <em>Statics and
    Structures</em> (stress, strain, why beams bend); from the intermediate tier,
    <em>Forces, Beams and Bridges</em> supplies the beam theory this lesson's
    performance indices are derived from, and <em>Units, Estimates and
    Back-of-Envelope</em> is the habit behind every sanity check below.
    <em>Thermodynamics for Engineers</em> returns wherever service temperature
    enters a constraint.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>translate a design brief into function, objective, constraints and free variables;</li>
    <li>derive and use performance indices such as E^½/ρ for a light stiff beam;</li>
    <li>read an Ashby chart — bubbles, guideline lines, property envelopes — as a complete ranking;</li>
    <li>compute thermal stress σ = E·α·ΔT and pick materials that survive thermal shock;</li>
    <li>fold shape factor, processing limits and documentation into a material decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every engineering decision includes an invisible question: <em>what should it be made
    of?</em> — answered, historically, by tradition (bronze for statues, steel for bridges)
    and by iteration. Mike Ashby's method made it systematic: <strong>translate the design
    into property requirements, screen the material universe against hard limits, rank the
    survivors by a performance index, and read the trade-offs off charts</strong> where every
    material class occupies its own bubble. The syllabus's property families — physical and
    phase behaviour, mechanical, chemical, thermal, electrical — are exactly the columns of
    the selector's database; each gets its moment in this lesson, and then the selection
    machinery turns them into decisions. The materials ladder built these properties lesson
    by lesson; this one is where they are spent.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="translate, screen, chart, document" widget="equationStep" config={matSelCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels are strictly ordered — you
    cannot screen before you have translated the design into limits, and you cannot
    rank before screening has disqualified the failures. Panel 2 carries the key
    distinction the quiz will test: hard limits are pass/fail, ranking objectives
    are optimisation. Everything else in this lesson is these four steps, expanded.
  </p>

  <h2>The property families: what the database holds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Physical and phase behaviour:</strong> density, melting point, and the phase
    stability landscape — where the phase-diagram lesson's maps enter selection as the
    question "is this microstructure stable at service temperature?" (superalloys exist
    because their strengthening phase survives 1,000 °C; polymers largely do not enter
    hot sections because nothing about them does).</li>
    <li><strong>Mechanical:</strong> elastic modulus E, yield strength σ_y, fracture
    toughness K_IC, fatigue endurance — the failure lesson's numbers. Toughness and
    strength trade against each other in real tables of data, and the selection method's
    job is to make the trade explicit rather than optimistic.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermal:</strong> conductivity k, expansion coefficient α, specific heat,
    maximum service temperature. Conductivity spans five decades — copper ≈ 400 W/(m·K),
    steel ≈ 50, window glass ≈ 1, polymers ≈ 0.2, insulation foams ≈ 0.03 — which is why
    the same chart picks heat sinks and duvets. Expansion mismatch is the quiet killer of
    joined structures (a glass-to-metal seal cracks because the partners expand
    differently), and expansion times stiffness times temperature swing gives thermal
    stress, quantified below.</li>
    <li><strong>Electrical:</strong> conductivity or resistivity, dielectric strength,
    magnetic behaviour — copper's 59 MS/m conductivity is why it <em>is</em> the wire, and
    insulators are chosen almost as deliberately (tracking resistance, dielectric loss).
    But conductivity per unit mass reshuffles the ranking: aluminium's 38 MS/m is only
    two-thirds of copper's, yet divided by density (2.7 versus 8.9 Mg/m³) aluminium
    carries more than twice the current per kilogram — the reason overhead transmission
    lines are aluminium (steel-cored for strength), not copper.</li>
    <li><strong>Chemical:</strong> corrosion and oxidation resistance, UV and solvent
    compatibility, galvanic compatibility. The galvanic series ranks metals by noble
    potential; connecting a less-noble metal to a more-noble one in an electrolyte makes
    the less-noble one the sacrificial anode — useful (galvanised steel: zinc, at
    −0.76 V standard potential versus iron's −0.44 V, corrodes so the steel doesn't) and
    fatal (steel fasteners in copper sheet, +0.34 V, dissolve). Corrosion is chemistry with
    a failure lesson attached.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Two of these numbers deserve the full worked treatment. <strong>Thermal stress</strong>
    is σ = E·α·ΔT: a continuous-welded steel rail (E = 210 GPa, α = 12 × 10⁻⁶ per kelvin)
    prevented from expanding over a 40 K swing carries 210 × 10⁹ × 12 × 10⁻⁶ × 40 ≈
    100 MPa — a third of its yield strength, spent doing nothing but existing at the wrong
    temperature. Railroads manage the budget by fastening rails at a chosen "neutral"
    temperature so summer and winter excursions split evenly. <strong>Thermal shock</strong>
    is the same equation with a gradient: a surface suddenly cooled contracts while the
    interior does not, and the surface cracks if the induced stress reaches σ_f, which
    happens at ΔT_c ≈ σ_f/(E·α) — the thermal-shock index. Low expansion is the whole
    trick: borosilicate glass (α ≈ 3.3 × 10⁻⁶) survives ΔT ≈ 240 K where soda-lime
    (α ≈ 9 × 10⁻⁶) manages about 70 K — why ovenware is borosilicate and windows are not,
    and why fused silica (α ≈ 0.5 × 10⁻⁶) survives the extreme end.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pin the thermal-shock survival points on the scale" widget="place" config={msShockPlace} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "copper is always the right wire metal — it
    conducts best." Per cubic metre that is true (59 against aluminium's 38 MS/m),
    but per kilogram the ranking flips: aluminium's density is low enough that it
    carries more than twice the current of copper per unit mass, which is why
    overhead transmission lines are aluminium (steel-cored for strength). The belief
    is reasonable because "best conductor" is the property you notice first — but a
    selection compares the property <em>combination</em> the objective asks for. The
    correct picture: conductivity per unit cost picks house wiring; conductivity per
    unit mass picks power lines; the database answers only the question you write down.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each selection basis with the wire it picks" widget="match" config={msWireMatch} />

  <h2>Translation: from adjectives to indices</h2>
  <p>
    The method's first move converts design language into function, objective, constraints
    and free variables. Take the canonical case: a <strong>light, stiff beam</strong> of
    length L, stiffness S, cross-section A — material and shape free. Beam theory gives
    stiffness S = C·E·I/L³ with C a load-configuration constant and I the second moment of
    area; for geometrically similar sections I scales with A², so A = √(S·L³/(C·E)). Mass
    is m = ρ·A·L = ρ·L·√(S·L³/(C·E)). Everything except ρ and E is fixed by the
    specification, so minimising mass means maximising <strong>M = E^½/ρ</strong> — the
    <em>performance index</em>, and the reason the exponent depends on loading: a tension
    tie gives M = E/ρ, a panel in bending E^⅓/ρ. The method's power is that the design
    speaks in indices; the material database answers in numbers.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="derive the index step by step" widget="order" config={msIndexOrder} />
  <p>
    Worked ranking for M = E^½/ρ, in (GPa)^½ per (Mg/m³): mild steel
    (E = 210, ρ = 7.8): 14.5/7.8 ≈ <strong>1.9</strong>. Titanium (116, 4.5): 10.8/4.5 ≈
    <strong>2.4</strong>. Aluminium (69, 2.7): 8.3/2.7 ≈ <strong>3.1</strong>. Carbon-fibre
    composite (E ≈ 100 along the fibre, ρ = 1.55): 10/1.55 ≈ <strong>6.5</strong>. Pine,
    along the grain (E ≈ 10, ρ = 0.5): 3.2/0.5 ≈ <strong>6.3</strong>. So for light stiff
    <em>bending</em>, wood genuinely rivals carbon fibre and crushes steel — evolution
    optimised trees for exactly this index — while for a tension tie (E/ρ) the ranking
    reshuffles (CFRP ~65, wood ~20, steel ~27, aluminium ~26) and geometry (thin-walled
    tubes) re-enters. Change the objective to cost per unit stiffness and steel surges
    back; the winner is a property of the <em>question</em>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file the worked ranking by its numbers" widget="sort-buckets" config={msRankBuckets} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> translation converts the brief into a performance
    index, and the worked ranking shows the punchline already — the winner depends
    on the loading geometry and the objective, not on any material's resume. What
    remains is to perform that ranking across all 100,000 candidates at once, which
    is what the charts do.
  </p>

  <h2>Ashby charts: the whole database as a picture</h2>
  <p>
    Plot modulus against density for 100,000 materials on log-log axes and the point cloud
    organises into class bubbles: ceramics top-right (stiff, dense-ish), foams bottom-left
    (compliant, light), elastomers far bottom-left, metals in a diagonal band. Two
    graphical tools turn this into selection. <strong>Guideline lines</strong>: a line of
    slope 2 (from log E = 2 log ρ + const) marks constant E^½/ρ; slide it across the chart
    toward the top-left and the first bubble it touches is the winner — an entire ranking,
    performed as geometry. <strong>Property envelopes</strong>: bubbles are honest about
    scatter (ceramics' strength spans two decades because flaws dominate — the fracture
    lesson's statistics made visible). The same chart pair with other axes (strength vs
    density, thermal expansion vs conductivity, cost vs embodied carbon) extends the
    picture to every objective family; the framework even displays limits (maximum service
    temperature as a horizontal ceiling that knocks out polymers below it).
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each class bubble with its address on the chart" widget="match" config={msBubbleMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the "best material" exists. Best is index-dependent and
    constraint-dependent: carbon fibre wins light-stiff bending and loses cost-per-stiffness;
    steel wins cheap-stiff and loses light-anything; glass wins thermal shock
    <em>if</em> it is borosilicate (index σ_f/(E·α) ≈ 240 K for borosilicate versus
    ~70 K for soda-lime — why ovenware is borosilicate and windows are not). Selection
    discipline means writing the objective down before falling in love with a material.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the translation step turns adjectives into indices, and
    the charts turn a database of 100,000 materials into a ranking you can perform
    as geometry — with the standing lesson that "best" belongs to the question, not
    the material. Two multipliers remain before the answer is buildable.
  </p>

  <h2>Shape, process, and the rest of the workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Two multipliers refine the index answer. <strong>Shape</strong>: the index method
    assumes a fixed section; real sections vary, and the <em>shape factor</em> captures how
    much — an I-beam or a box section carries roughly 10–25× the bending stiffness of the
    solid rod of the same mass (bounded by local buckling), so "steel, shaped well" can
    beat "aluminium, shaped badly". <strong>Process</strong>: every candidate carries a
    process compatibility list — castable, weldable, extrudable, printable — and process
    sets the achievable microstructure (the heat-treatment lesson's itineraries) and hence
    the properties in the table. The final steps are documentation and iteration: record
    which materials passed, why the winner won, and what would change the answer — the audit
    trail that lets the next engineer (or the next failure investigation) see the decision's
    logic.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the magnitude — the shape multiplier" widget="estimate" config={msShapeEstimate} />
  <p>
    Run the whole workflow once, quickly, on a real object: a <strong>bicycle
    frame</strong>. Translation: it is a set of ties and beams loaded in bending and
    torsion; objective, minimum mass at acceptable cost; constraints, survive decades of
    fatigue, no rust, joinable, serviceable to −20 °C. Screening kills thermoplastics
    (creep), ceramics (toughness) and lead (weight). Ranking on E^½/ρ puts CFRP, titanium
    and aluminium at the top, steel below — which is exactly the market you observe:
    carbon-fibre racing frames, aluminium mid-range, titanium boutique, steel touring
    (where comfort, repairability and cost, not mass, are the implicit objectives — a
    translation the buyers made without ever writing it down). Documentation is the
    manufacturer's layup schedule or the tube supplier's certificate. One pass of the
    method, and the showroom becomes a chart you can read.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="one pass of the method — the bicycle frame" widget="branch" config={msFrameBranch} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "picking the right material is the whole job —
    once the index names a winner, the design is done." The index method itself
    assumes a fixed section; real performance then depends on the <em>shape</em> the
    material is worked into (an I-beam carries roughly 10–25× the bending stiffness
    of the same mass of rod, so steel shaped well beats aluminium shaped badly) and
    on the <em>process</em>, which sets the achievable microstructure and hence the
    very properties the table lists. A selection that ignores process compatibility
    can rank a material that cannot be welded into the joint it is meant to hold.
    Material, shape and process are decided together — the showroom exercise above
    is one pass of all three.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the three moves of the selection method in order, and say what each one rejects or ranks.</li>
    <li>A steel rail (E = 210 GPa, α = 12 × 10⁻⁶ per kelvin) is prevented from expanding through a 40 K swing. What stress develops, and what fraction of a typical yield strength is that?</li>
    <li>Why does wood rival carbon fibre for light stiff beams but lose badly for light stiff ties?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Translate the design into function, objective,
    constraints and free variables; screen against hard limits (disqualifying
    candidates outright); rank the survivors by a performance index (ordering what
    remains) — then chart, shape, process and document. (2) σ = E·α·ΔT ≈ 210 × 10⁹ ×
    12 × 10⁻⁶ × 40 ≈ 100 MPa — about a third of steel's yield strength, which is why
    railroads fasten rails at a neutral temperature. (3) Bending rewards E^½/ρ,
    where wood's very low density shines (6.3 versus steel's 1.9); tension rewards
    E/ρ, where the low density stops helping (wood ~20 against CFRP ~65). Same
    materials, different loading geometry, different exponent.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Selection is a method, not a preference: translate, screen, rank, document.</li>
    <li>Hard limits disqualify; ranking objectives order the survivors — confusing the two ruins the answer.</li>
    <li>Performance indices (E^½/ρ for beams, E/ρ for ties) encode the loading geometry in the exponent.</li>
    <li>Ashby charts turn a database into geometry: bubbles, guideline lines, property envelopes.</li>
    <li>"Best" is a property of the question — objective and constraints decide, not the material alone.</li>
    <li>Thermal stress σ = E·α·ΔT and the shock index σ_f/(E·α) make temperature a selection criterion.</li>
    <li>Shape and process are multipliers: a shaped section and a compatible process can overturn the index ranking.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The control-systems and signal-processing lessons complete this advanced tier
    with the dynamic side of engineering — the behaviour of systems once built,
    rather than the choice of what to build them from. And the pressure-vessel
    conflict above is the bridge to failure analysis: two failure modes always mean
    two indices.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Indices that fight back.</strong> The pressure vessel is Ashby's showcase of
    conflicting indices. Mass minimisation wants the wall as thin as the yield strength
    allows — maximise σ_y/ρ. Safety wants the vessel to <em>yield before it breaks</em>,
    so any crack present is still shorter than the size at which the (now known) stress
    reaches K_IC — maximise K_IC/σ_y. The two indices point in opposite directions:
    ultra-high-strength steel wins the first and fails the second (its toughness is poor
    precisely because its strength is high), while low-strength, very tough steel does
    the reverse. The professional answer is a middle-strength, high-toughness steel — or
    titanium when mass is precious enough (spacecraft tanks) — and the general lesson is
    that a design with two failure modes needs two indices, and the candidate set is the
    region where both are satisfied. Failure-mode thinking is what stops the index method
    from ranking confidently into catastrophe.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="one line of the vessel decision is wrong — find it" widget="spot-error" config={msVesselSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>Multi-objective selection.</strong> Real designs optimise several objectives at
    once — mass <em>and</em> cost <em>and</em> embodied carbon. The rigorous treatment
    turns them into one penalty function via <strong>exchange constants</strong>: how many
    kilograms is one dollar worth, on this design? (For aircraft, mass is expensive enough
    that the exchange constant justifies titanium and CFRP; for a ladder, it does not.)
    Ashby's charts extended to embodied energy and CO₂ have shifted rankings at the
    industry scale — timber construction's rise is partly an index event: add carbon as an
    objective and structural wood beats concrete and steel on stiffness per tonne of CO₂,
    which is the method converting a cultural preference into an engineering argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the data is soft.</strong> Material property databases are compilations,
    and every number carries provenance and scatter: ceramic strength is Weibull-distributed
    (a "strength" is a probability, not a value), composite properties are direction-dependent
    by factors of ten, polymer properties creep with temperature and time. Professional
    practice uses allowables (A- and B-basis statistical limits from certification testing)
    rather than datasheet typicals — the difference between a brochure and a design value.
    Selection quality is therefore bounded by data quality, which is why documentation of
    sources is not bureaucracy but engineering.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Machine learning now augments the method — generative
    alloy design and property prediction from composition extend screening to materials not
    yet made, with validation (the benchmarking lesson's contamination problem, in a new
    costume) the open issue. Multi-material and graded structures (3-D printed functionally
    graded parts) escape the single-bubble assumption entirely: when a part is two
    materials with a gradient between them, what is "the material"? And circular-economy
    objectives (disassembly, recyclability, recycled-content property penalties) are still
    finding their rigorous index form — the next decade of the method is being written in
    sustainability units.
  </p>

